<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>SparrowKit</title>
    <%#
      Inlined rather than linked. A mountable engine cannot assume the host has
      an asset pipeline, or which one, and this page has to render on a fresh
      install before anything is configured.

      The font URL is rewritten on the way in. Inlined CSS resolves a relative
      url() against the PAGE, not against a stylesheet, so the compiled
      `url(InterVariable.woff2)` asked the host for /InterVariable.woff2 --
      a routing error in the log on every single console page, and the theme
      quietly falling back to a system font.

      It cannot be absolute in the compiled file: the host picks where to mount
      this engine and the build cannot know. Here it can, because
      sparrow_ui.font_path is generated against the actual mount.
    %>
    <style><%=
      File.read(SparrowUi.stylesheet_path)
        .sub(SparrowUi::FONT_URL_IN_STYLESHEET, "url(#{sparrow_ui.font_path})")
        .html_safe
    %></style>

    <script>
      // Applies a saved theme choice before anything is painted.
      //
      // In <head>, above <body>, and deliberately not deferred. Anywhere later
      // -- the end of the body, DOMContentLoaded, an external file -- the
      // browser has already painted the page dark, and somebody who chose
      // light watches it flash dark and correct itself on every navigation.
      //
      // Nothing here is required for the console to work. Stored nothing, or
      // scripting off, and the page is dark: that is the default, and it comes
      // from the stylesheet rather than from this script.
      (function () {
        try {
          var choice = localStorage.getItem("sparrowkit-console-theme");
          // "dark" is written through as well as light and system, even though
          // it is what the page would do anyway. The attribute is then a
          // record of what was CHOSEN rather than of what it looks like, and
          // the day the default changes a developer who picked dark still has
          // dark.
          if (choice === "light" || choice === "dark" || choice === "system") {
            document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", choice);
          }
        } catch (e) {
          // Storage can throw rather than return null: Safari in private
          // browsing, and any browser with site data blocked. A console that
          // refused to render because it could not remember a colour would be
          // a poor trade, so the default stands.
        }
      })();
    </script>
  </head>
  <body class="min-h-screen bg-slate-50 text-slate-900 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100">
    <%#
      The mount prefix, worked out once.

      `request.script_name` is wrong here and right on the hub, which is the
      trap: on the hub it is sparrow_ui's own mount, but this layout also
      renders inside every panel's engine, where it is that panel's mount --
      so nav links built from it would point at /sparrowkit/auth/auth.
      sparrow_ui.root_path is asked of the route set that did the mounting and
      is the same string on every page.

      chomp("/") because root_path ends in one and panel.path begins with one.
      Mounted at the host's root it chomps to "" and the links are still right.
    %>
    <% console_root = sparrow_ui.root_path.chomp("/") %>

    <header class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-white dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
      <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-4xl flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-3 px-6 py-4">
        <a href="<%= sparrow_ui.root_path %>"
           class="text-base font-semibold text-purple-900 dark:text-purple-300">SparrowKit</a>
        <span class="rounded-full bg-slate-100 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-slate-800 dark:text-slate-300">
          <%= SparrowUi::VERSION %>
        </span>

        <%#
          The nav and the switcher, wrapped together as ONE flex item so that
          `ml-auto` has something to push.

          Both tried it on their own first, and both were wrong. On the nav
          alone, a window around 640px is wide enough to keep the nav on the
          first row and too narrow for the switcher, which then wraps to a
          second row carrying no auto margin and lands hard left. On the
          switcher alone it is worse: the nav is not rendered at all when no
          module has registered, and the only right-aligned thing on the header
          is gone.

          Wrapped, the pair right-aligns as a unit and `justify-end` keeps them
          against the edge when they wrap against each other.
        %>
        <% panels = SparrowUi::Console.panels %>
        <%#
          Which panel this is, asked once. The nav asks the same question per
          link to mark the current one; the Docs button needs the answer itself,
          and computing it twice is two places for the two to disagree about
          where you are.
        %>
        <% current_panel = panels.find { |panel| panel.current?(request.path, console_root) } %>
        <div class="ml-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-end gap-x-5 gap-y-3">
          <%#
            From the registry, so this row is whatever is actually installed. A
            hardcoded Auth | Mail | Pay would offer a link to a gem that is not
            in the Gemfile, and 404 the developer who trusted it.
          %>
          <% if panels.any? %>
            <nav aria-label="Modules" class="flex items-center gap-1">
              <% panels.each do |panel| %>
                <% href = "#{console_root}#{panel.path}" %>
                <% current = panel.current?(request.path, console_root) %>
                <%#
                  The full name as a tooltip, but only where it says something
                  the link does not. Mail's short name IS its name, and a title
                  repeating the visible text is a second copy of the same word
                  for anything that reads both aloud.
                %>
                <%#
                  Asked here as well as on the hub's cards, so the hub computes
                  each module's status twice per render. Left that way on
                  purpose: Panel#status is documented as asked fresh every time,
                  precisely so a panel that was just saved is not reported by a
                  stale copy, and every module's answer is a read of credentials
                  Rails has already memoised. Caching it per request would buy
                  nothing measurable and put a way for the two to disagree into
                  the one page that shows both.
                %>
                <a href="<%= href %>"
                   <%= "aria-current=\"page\"".html_safe if current %>
                   <%= tag.attributes(title: panel.name) if panel.short_name != panel.name %>
                   class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-2.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-slate-600 hover:bg-slate-100 hover:text-purple-900 aria-[current=page]:bg-slate-100 aria-[current=page]:text-purple-900 dark:text-slate-300 dark:hover:bg-slate-800 dark:hover:text-purple-300 dark:aria-[current=page]:bg-slate-800 dark:aria-[current=page]:text-purple-300"><%#
                  The glyph AFTER the name, and that is about the reading order
                  rather than the look. The partial ends with the state in
                  words for a screen reader, so putting it first would make the
                  link announce ": Ready Auth".
                %><%= panel.short_name %><%= render "sparrow_ui/console/nav_status", status: panel.status %></a>
              <% end %>
            </nav>
          <% end %>

          <%#
            Documentation, for whichever module is open. The URL comes from the
            panel rather than being written out per page: Panel#docs_url already
            derives one from the key, so a page that spelled its own out would
            be a second copy to keep in step.

            Opens in a new tab, which is not the usual default here. The auth
            and mail panels are forms somebody may be halfway through filling
            in, and sending them off to another site in the same tab throws
            away what they had typed.
          %>
          <% docs_href = current_panel&.docs_url || "#{SparrowUi::Console::DOCS_URL}/" %>
          <a href="<%= docs_href %>"
             target="_blank"
             rel="noopener"
             class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-2.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-slate-600 hover:bg-slate-100 hover:text-purple-900 dark:text-slate-300 dark:hover:bg-slate-800 dark:hover:text-purple-300">
            Docs<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
            <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" class="size-3.5">
              <path d="M6 3H3.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 0 2 4.5v8A1.5 1.5 0 0 0 3.5 14h8a1.5 1.5 0 0 0 1.5-1.5V10" stroke-linecap="round"/>
              <path d="M10 2h4v4M14 2 7.5 8.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            </svg>
          </a>

          <%#
            Hidden until the script un-hides it, because without scripting
            there is nothing for it to do: the page is already dark by default,
            from the stylesheet, and a dead control that never responds is
            worse than no control.

            Hidden by the `hidden` UTILITY rather than the attribute. The
            attribute is `display: none` from the browser's own stylesheet,
            which any author rule beats -- so `flex` on the same element would
            win and it would never be hidden at all. Both are classes here, so
            the last one set decides.

            Styled off aria-pressed rather than a class the script swaps. The
            state a screen reader announces and the state you can see are then
            the same fact, and cannot drift apart.

            Icons rather than words, and each still carries its word. The glyph
            is hidden from anything that reads the page aloud and the name is
            in an sr-only span, so the button is still announced as "System",
            "Light" or "Dark" -- an icon-only control whose name is only in a
            tooltip is a control a screen reader cannot describe. `title` is
            there as well, for a pointer.
          %>
          <div id="sk-theme"
               role="group"
               aria-label="Colour theme"
               class="hidden items-center gap-0.5 rounded-md border border-slate-300 p-0.5 dark:border-slate-700">
            <button type="button" data-sk-theme="system" aria-pressed="false" title="Follow the system setting"
                    class="rounded p-1.5 text-slate-600 hover:text-slate-900 aria-pressed:bg-slate-100 aria-pressed:text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-300 dark:hover:text-slate-100 dark:aria-pressed:bg-slate-800 dark:aria-pressed:text-slate-100">
              <span class="sr-only">System</span>
              <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" class="size-4">
                <rect x="1.75" y="2.75" width="12.5" height="8.5" rx="1.25"/>
                <path d="M5.5 14h5M8 11.5V14" stroke-linecap="round"/>
              </svg>
            </button>
            <button type="button" data-sk-theme="light" aria-pressed="false" title="Always light"
                    class="rounded p-1.5 text-slate-600 hover:text-slate-900 aria-pressed:bg-slate-100 aria-pressed:text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-300 dark:hover:text-slate-100 dark:aria-pressed:bg-slate-800 dark:aria-pressed:text-slate-100">
              <span class="sr-only">Light</span>
              <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" class="size-4">
                <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="3.25"/>
                <path d="M8 1v1.5M8 13.5V15M15 8h-1.5M2.5 8H1M12.95 3.05l-1.06 1.06M4.11 11.89l-1.06 1.06M12.95 12.95l-1.06-1.06M4.11 4.11 3.05 3.05" stroke-linecap="round"/>
              </svg>
            </button>
            <button type="button" data-sk-theme="dark" aria-pressed="false" title="Always dark"
                    class="rounded p-1.5 text-slate-600 hover:text-slate-900 aria-pressed:bg-slate-100 aria-pressed:text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-300 dark:hover:text-slate-100 dark:aria-pressed:bg-slate-800 dark:aria-pressed:text-slate-100">
              <span class="sr-only">Dark</span>
              <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" class="size-4">
                <path d="M13.5 9.6A5.75 5.75 0 0 1 6.4 2.5a5.75 5.75 0 1 0 7.1 7.1Z" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
              </svg>
            </button>
          </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-10">
      <% if flash[:notice] %>
        <div role="status"
             class="mb-6 rounded-lg border border-green-200 bg-green-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-green-800 dark:border-green-900 dark:bg-green-950 dark:text-green-300">
          <%= flash[:notice] %>
        </div>
      <% end %>
      <% if flash[:alert] %>
        <div role="alert"
             class="mb-6 rounded-lg border border-red-200 bg-red-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-red-800 dark:border-red-900 dark:bg-red-950 dark:text-red-300">
          <%= flash[:alert] %>
        </div>
      <% end %>

      <%= yield %>
    </main>

    <%#
      In the layout, so it reaches every page under /sparrowkit -- the hub and
      every module's panel, including ones no gem has written yet. A panel that
      had to remember to render its own footer is a panel that will forget.

      The year is the current one rather than a literal. A copyright line
      frozen at the year it was typed is wrong from the next January onwards,
      and nobody notices a footer going stale.
    %>
    <footer class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 pb-10 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-300">
      &copy; <%= Time.now.year %>
      <a href="https://sparrowkit.co"
         class="font-medium text-purple-900 hover:underline dark:text-purple-300">SparrowKit</a>
      - a
      <a href="https://sparrowsoft.co"
         class="font-medium text-purple-900 hover:underline dark:text-purple-300">SparrowSoft</a>
      project
    </footer>

    <script>
      // The other half of the switcher. The <head> script already applied the
      // stored choice; this reveals the control and lets it be changed.
      //
      // At the end of the body rather than in <head> so the buttons exist to
      // be found, and with no listener to wait for. Vanilla and inline, for
      // the same reason as the rest of this console: it is served to one
      // machine in development, and a toolchain to toggle a colour would be
      // the wrong trade.
      (function () {
        var group = document.getElementById("sk-theme");
        if (!group) return;

        var buttons = group.querySelectorAll("[data-sk-theme]");
        var root = document.documentElement;

        // "dark" for anything unrecognised, because dark is the default and
        // the button that is lit has to be the state the page is actually in.
        function read() {
          try {
            var stored = localStorage.getItem("sparrowkit-console-theme");
            return stored === "light" || stored === "system" || stored === "dark"
              ? stored
              : "dark";
          } catch (e) {
            // Same storage failure the <head> script tolerates. The control
            // still works for this page; it just will not be remembered, and
            // saying so would be a dialog nobody asked for.
            return "dark";
          }
        }

        function show(choice) {
          for (var i = 0; i < buttons.length; i++) {
            var button = buttons[i];
            button.setAttribute(
              "aria-pressed",
              button.getAttribute("data-sk-theme") === choice ? "true" : "false"
            );
          }
        }

        // All three are written, none removed. The absence of the attribute is
        // not a fourth state to fall back to -- it is the default, dark -- so
        // clearing it on "system" would leave a developer on a light-set
        // machine looking at a dark page having just asked for their machine's
        // setting.
        function apply(choice) {
          root.setAttribute("data-theme", choice);

          try {
            localStorage.setItem("sparrowkit-console-theme", choice);
          } catch (e) {
            // Nothing to do. The page still changed.
          }

          show(choice);
        }

        for (var i = 0; i < buttons.length; i++) {
          buttons[i].addEventListener("click", function (event) {
            apply(event.currentTarget.getAttribute("data-sk-theme"));
          });
        }

        show(read());

        // Both classes, in this order. `hidden` and `flex` are each a
        // `display` rule from the same stylesheet, so removing one and adding
        // the other is what actually reveals it.
        group.classList.remove("hidden");
        group.classList.add("flex");
      })();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
